activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4215886Z 5. Create the pull request with the create_pull_request tool from safeoutputs exactly once, only when the final title/body/branch are ready.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4217547Z 6. After calling `create_pull_request`, STOP. Do not probe with another PR call, do not test auth with manual `git push`, and do not rewrite git remotes.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4218469Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4221018Z **Important**: The `branch` parameter in the create_pull_request tool **must exactly match the name of your current local git branch** — the branch you just committed to. You can verify this with `git branch --show-current`. Never invent or guess a branch name; always use the actual branch name from `git branch --show-current`. If you are on an existing branch (e.g. you checked out a PR branch), use that branch name.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4225874Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4227454Z **Important**: `create_pull_request` is not a sandbox. Every successful call records a real PR intent that can become an externally visible pull request. Never use placeholder payloads like `"test"` / `"test"` to see what happens. If you are not ready to open the real PR, use `noop` or `report_incomplete` instead.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4229417Z </safe-output-tools>
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4229878Z <mcp-clis>
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4230931Z The following servers are available as CLI executables on `PATH`. Invoke them from bash - they are **not** MCP tools; do not call them via an MCP tool interface.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4232122Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4232529Z - `safeoutputs` — run `safeoutputs --help` to see available tools
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4233021Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4233381Z For `safeoutputs` and `mcpscripts`, always use the CLI commands above.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4233900Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4234997Z For `safeoutputs`, every successful call is a real write-intent declaration - do not use it for probing, auth checks, or placeholder payloads. Use `noop` or `report_incomplete` if not ready to emit the final action.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4236239Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4236686Z For multiple or complex arguments, pipe a JSON object on stdin using `.` as the sentinel:
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4237484Z ```bash
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4238083Z printf '{"item_number":42,"body":"### Title\n\nBody."}' | safeoutputs add_comment .
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4238979Z # or write to a file: safeoutputs create_pull_request . < /tmp/payload.json
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4239680Z ```
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4239884Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4240484Z To inject an entire local file as the `body` field without re-embedding its content in the model context, use `jq -Rs`:
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4241573Z ```bash
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4243710Z jq -Rs --arg discussion_number "$DISCUSSION_NUMBER" \
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4244568Z   '{discussion_number: ($discussion_number|tonumber), body: .}' \
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4245563Z   discussion-body.md \
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4246059Z   | safeoutputs update_discussion .
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4246579Z ```
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T15:29:52.4247882Z `jq -Rs` reads the file as a raw string (`-R`) and slurps it into a single JSON string value (`-s`), so `body` is always a valid JSON field. Piping `cat file | safeoutputs ...` does not populate `body` and will be rejected.
